Diagnosis: 4 of the 5 failures return **0 rows** while gold returns rows (sp0362, sp0536, sp0610, sp0894). The queries are structurally plausible, so the likely culprit is a wrong literal / wrong join key / wrong column semantics that silently filters everything out — and the incumbent never executes anything, so it cannot notice. Rather than adding more prompt rules (the frequent-pattern fix), I target this hidden weakness with an execution-grounded repair loop: draft → execute → if empty/error, feed back real sampled values from the referenced tables plus the error text and ask for one corrected query. This is generic (no task/db knowledge) and only spends the second policy call when the first draft is provably suspicious.

